::: text-justify
As we can see from above, Netflix stock 'overshadows' any subtle variations in the stock prices for the remaining companies/stocks, simply because of the difference in scales/range of values. <br> One way to remedy that is by clicking on NFLX tile in the legend, this will deselect the Netflix Series - pun intended - from the graph; a native functionality in `{Plotly}` Another way is to be faceting on companies, essentially a '`GROUP BY TICKER`' but for plots. This is what is shown next.
:::

::: {#cell-facetted-plots .cell execution_count=6}
``` {.python .cell-code}
def plot_stock_facets(data, title="Stock Price Performance", height=800):
    """
    create faceted line charts for stock prices - ggplot2 style
    """

    # get unique tickers for subplot layout
    tickers = sorted(data['ticker'].unique().to_list())
    n_tickers = len(tickers)

    # calculate subplot grid (2 columns)
    cols = 2
    rows = (n_tickers + cols - 1) // cols

    # create subplots with shared x-axis
    fig = make_subplots(
        rows=rows, 
        cols=cols,
        subplot_titles=[
          f"{name} ({ticker})" for name, ticker in zip(
            ['Apple', 'Google', 'Microsoft', 'Netflix', 'Nvidia', 'Tesla'],
            sorted(pl_results['ticker'].unique())
            )
            ],
        shared_xaxes=True,
        shared_yaxes=False, # essentially scales_y = 'free' in r
        vertical_spacing=0.08,
        horizontal_spacing=0.1
    )

    # color palette (ggplot2-like)
    colors = ['#1f77b4', '#ff7f0e', '#2ca02c', '#d62728', '#9467bd', '#8c564b']

    # add line for each ticker
    for i, ticker in enumerate(tickers):
        ticker_data = data.filter(pl.col('ticker') == ticker).sort('Date')

        row = (i // cols) + 1
        col = (i % cols) + 1

        fig.add_trace(
            go.Scatter(
                x=ticker_data['Date'].to_list(),
                y=ticker_data['Close'].to_list(),
                mode='lines',
                name=ticker,
                line=dict(color=colors[i % len(colors)], width=2),
                hovertemplate='<b>%{fullData.name}</b><br>' +
                            'Date: %{x}<br>' +
                            'Close: $%{y:.2f}<extra></extra>',
                showlegend=False  
            ),
            row=row, col=col
        )

    # apply ggplot2 theme
    theme = create_ggplot_theme()
    fig.update_layout(**theme)
    fig.update_layout(
        title=title,
        height=height,
        showlegend=False
    )

    # apply axis styling
    fig = update_axes_theme(fig)

    # update subplot titles to be more prominent
    for i, annotation in enumerate(fig['layout']['annotations']):
        annotation['font'] = dict(
          size=14,
          color='#333333',
          family='Merriweather, Georgia, serif'
          )

    fig.update_layout(
      title={
        'text': title,
        'x': 0.5,
        'xanchor': 'center',
        'font': {'size': 16, 'family': 'Merriweather, Georgia, serif', 'color': '#333333', 'weight': 'bold'}
        }
    )
    return fig

# faceted plot 
faceted_chart = plot_stock_facets(
    pl_results, 
    title="Stock Performance by Ticker (6 Months)", 
    height=600
)
faceted_chart.show()
